// raster's coverage, and the crash the last three points were hiding.
//
//   wac task coverage:raster
//   wac task coverage:raster --verbose
//
// **299 of 299**, from 230 of 298, and both lists below are empty: every branch point is expected to
// be covered and the first one that is not fails by name. `issues/system/0205`.
//
// ## None of the package's 26 tests were being called
//
// `cov_exercise.wac` drove `test/wac/scenes.wac` and nothing else, so the number said what the scenes
// reached. Five of the tests take `(Core, Cli)` — they read the vendored font hex beside the generated
// table — and until `issues/system/0221` a driver could not call one at all. Wiring them took
// `grid.wac` from 69 of 128 to 114: the scenes drive a `Grid` the way a desktop does, and the tests
// drive it the way a terminal does.
//
// **`hosts_test.wac`'s one test is left out deliberately.** It draws the same desktop on two hosts and
// compares the pixels, which means the drawing happens in *child processes*: the driver would pay its
// six seconds and measure only the spawn. A test whose subject runs somewhere else contributes nothing
// to a coverage figure taken here — which is a different reason from `packages/http`'s and worth not
// conflating with it.
//
// ## The twenty-two that were left were the degenerate contracts
//
// Almost every remaining point was a guard at the top of a method, each with a sentence in its doc
// comment saying what happens and nothing running it: `close` an index that is not there, `selectTo`
// before `selectFrom`, `toFocused` with no windows, a rectangle with no area, `Grid.create` handed a
// zero or a negative. A window manager reaches these in the ordinary course of being used.
//
// The one worth naming is the **backwards selection**. `Grid`'s field comment says `selHead` may be
// before `selAnchor` — "a drag upwards is an ordinary drag" — and normalising at the point of use is
// recorded there as a decision. Nothing had ever dragged upwards, so both halves of the two min/max
// ternaries implementing it had never run.
//
// ## And one real crash
//
// `grid.wac:228` clamps the cursor when it lands past the last column, and reaching it needs a glyph
// wider than the whole grid: `put` wraps *before* writing, so after a wrap `curCol` is 0 and only a
// double-width glyph in a **one-column** grid can still overshoot. Writing that test trapped —
// *array element access out of bounds* — because the continuation cell went one past the end of the
// row, which on the last row is one past the end of the array.
//
// `Grid.create` clamps `cols` up to 1 rather than refusing, and `Desk.open` takes its column count
// from a caller, so a one-column window plus any double-width character brought the program down. The
// tail is guarded now and the code point keeps its single cell — the renderer already clips at the
// right edge, and losing input silently is the worse of the two wrong answers.
//
// **That is what an uncovered defensive clamp was worth here**: not a line to pin, but the one input
// nobody had built, and it crashed.
//
// ## The canaries
//
// Dropping the grants takes this to **298 of 299** — one point, which is worth writing down because I
// guessed ten before running it. The five tests that read the font hex check the *generated table
// against the vendored source*, which is a strong check and almost entirely a check of data rather
// than of branches; the scenes already reach the code they run through. Grants are not worth the same
// everywhere, and the number is the only way to know: nineteen in `packages/http`, twenty in
// `packages/regex`, a hundred and four in `packages/bignum`, one here.
//
// Removing the test calls from the exercise puts it back to 230 of 298, which is where this started.
// Neither list below may hold anything, so a new uncovered branch fails by name rather than shrinking
// a percentage.

import { Core, Cli } from "std/platform.wac";
import { Pin, Rule, Measured, measure, report, ratchet } from "../../../tools/wac/covledger.wac";

const string EXERCISE = "packages/raster/test/cov_exercise.wac";
const string PREFIX = "packages/raster/";

/** What the tests need: the vendored font hex, read from disk. */
string[] grants() { return string[]("--allow-read"); }

export i32 main(Core core, Cli cli) {
  bool verbose = false;
  i32 argc = cli.argCount().wait();
  for (i32 i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
    if (string.fromBytes(cli.arg(i).wait()) == "--verbose") { verbose = true; }
  }

  Measured m = measure(core, cli, EXERCISE, grants(), string[0](fill: ""));
  if (m.nPoints == 0) { return 1; }

  report(core, m.points, m.nPoints, m.counts, PREFIX, verbose);
  return ratchet(core, cli, m.points, m.nPoints, m.counts, PREFIX,
                 Pin[0](fill: Pin("", 0, "", true, "")),
                 Rule[0](fill: Rule("", "", "line", true, ""))) ? 1 : 0;
}
